 The general-purpose RISC-V x registers are builtin state for IDL (rather than being declared state). This avoids needing special language support (e.g., operator overloading) or verbose function calls on every x register access. Three special cases apply:
 
-1. The x0 register is hardwired to 0.
+1. The x0 register is hardwired to 0. Writes to x0 are ignored, and reads from it always return `XLEN'd0`.
 2. All writes to an x register when `MXLEN != XLEN` are sign-extended to `MXLEN`.
 3. All reads from an x register when `MXLEN != XLEN` ignore the upper bits of the register.
 
